    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's just really frustrating because it feels like they're specifically targeting the plants that are doing the best. it feels almost intentional, like they're hiding in the shadows or the soil and then just picking off the leaves when no one is around. do you think there's any way to set up something to catch them in the act? like a trap or something that shows where they're coming from?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you're dealing with a very stealthy infestation, which is frustrating! If they are targeting your strongest plants, it suggests they are highly efficient feeders, perhaps even more specialized pests. You can try setting up yellow sticky traps near the affected plants, as these are excellent for catching flying or crawling pests like fungus gnats or whiteflies before they cause major damage. These traps act like a beacon, showing you exactly where the population is congregating.\n\nIf the pests are definitely hiding in the soil, you might need to look into soil treatments or beneficial nematodes, which are microscopic organisms that hunt down root-dwelling pests. For a more direct visual, some growers use blue sticky traps, which can attract different types of pests than the yellow ones. These traps are your best bet for getting a \"snapshot\" of the hidden enemy!",
      "source": "target",
